## Detailed Port & Flow Analysis

The system contains three major components interacting over the network:

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph Windows PC [Windows Host / Server]
        ReactUI[React Frontend / Electron<br>Port: 3000 / Static]
        ExpressSrv[Express Backend API<br>Port: 5001]
        WebSocketSrv[WebSocket Server<br>Port: 8080]
    end
    
    subgraph RaspberryPi [Raspberry Pi <br> IP: 192.168.0.110]
        ScannerPy[Python Client BC/main.py<br>Port: 8000]
        SQLiteDB[SQLite: barcode_scans.db]
        Scanners[Physical HID Scanners]
    end

    %% Scans Flow
    Scanners -->|evdev keypress events| ScannerPy
    ScannerPy -->|Local SQLite Write| SQLiteDB
    ScannerPy -->|HTTP POST /api/barcode<br>IP: 192.168.0.141:5001| ExpressSrv
    
    %% Backend to Client Broadcast
    ExpressSrv -->|Local ws broadcast| WebSocketSrv
    WebSocketSrv -->|WebSockets update| ReactUI
    ReactUI -->|REST requests| ExpressSrv
```

### 1. Ports and Communication Matrix

| Source Component | Destination Component | Protocol / Port | Purpose | Target URL |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Physical Scanners** | **Python (Pi)** | USB HID (evdev) | Read scanner inputs as keycodes | `/dev/input/event*` |
| **Python (Pi)** | **Express Backend** | HTTP POST / `5001` | Transmit barcode scans in real-time | `http://192.168.0.141:5001/api/barcode` |
| **Python (Pi)** | **Internal loop** | Flask Thread / `8000` | Local API server for scans and health | `http://127.0.0.1:8000` |
| **React/Electron** | **Express Backend** | HTTP REST / `5001` | Admin login, database reconciliation, sync, settings | `http://localhost:5001/api` |
| **React/Electron** | **WebSocket Server** | WebSockets / `8080` | Listen for live barcode scan broadcasts | `ws://127.0.0.1:8080` |

### 2. Full Flow Analysis of a Scan Event
1. **Physical Scan**: A barcode scanner connected to the Raspberry Pi scans a barcode and acts as a USB keyboard.
2. **evdev Capture**: The Python script `scanner.py` running inside `main.py` detects keypresses via the `evdev` library, processes them, and prints them to the terminal.
3. **Database Insertion**: The scan is saved locally on the Raspberry Pi inside `/home/pi/BC/barcode_scans.db` (using the SQLite database manager).
4. **Network Transmission**: The Python client sends an HTTP POST request to the Node backend server running at `http://192.168.0.141:5001/api/barcode`.
5. **Deduplication & DB Save**: The Express server checks if the barcode is a duplicate within 200ms. If valid, it:
   - Stores the scan in MongoDB (`BCScan` collection).
   - Broadcasts the live scan update over WebSockets.
6. **Real-time UI Update**: The React app connected to `ws://127.0.0.1:8080` receives the scan payload and displays the scanned item instantly on the live dashboard.

